{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And you, go to the end</b> — the nun is in place of mem, and like it is <b>and you, take for yourself wheat</b> (Daniel 4:32); <b>upon it they shall bow down</b> (Isaiah 45:23); <b>others</b> (Daniel 7:24); and <b>he will not believe in life</b> — [the nun] in place of mem. Also the word Binyamin — Yefet ben Eli said: for this verse is Daniel's word to every maskil, that he should go on his way and not inquire about the end of days, until he to whom HaShem has decreed to bring the redeemer in his days finds it. And this is the meaning of <b>and you shall stand for your lot</b> (Daniel 12:13) — if it is decreed that you be alive. And this interpretation is far-fetched. And how very distinguished would the interpretation of the Gaon [Saadia] have been, had he told us what reason there is to stand in the lot with the end of days, for all of them are words of the angel and not words of Daniel. The book of Daniel is completed in Marcheshvan of the year four thousand nine hundred and ninety-six, in the city of Rhodes. And thanks to HaShem alone, who helped His servant Abraham — he is the author, he is the scribe."], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The comment addresses two linguistic points: the nun in לקץ (end) as a variant for a mem form, and a citation of Isaiah 45:23 where the verb יכרעון has nun instead of mem. The phrase 'ולא יאמין בחין' appears interruptive in context; translated hyper-literally as it stands. 'Also the word Binyamin' is obscure — possibly a separate lemma or textual corruption; rendered literally. The closing colophon is standard for Ibn Ezra's biblical commentaries."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:21:42.103145461Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}